landing-review: vulkan-only plan, landing 11-5e-3 -- the include residue

Range: main...HEAD (3f28f47..67271d9, 1 commit).

REVIEW DELIBERATELY SKIPPED, under CLAUDE.md *Review engine changes before they
land*: "Skip it for docs, scenarios, comments, or a few lines with no new state,
no new engine patch and no new GL object." This landing is the whole of that
clause and nothing else, and the reasons are checkable rather than asserted:

  - NO NEW STATE. Nothing is added. The code half of the diff is
    8 insertions / 6 deletions across three files, and six of those insertions
    are a comment and a whitespace split.
  - NO NEW ENGINE PATCH. No engine address appears on any added line
    (`git diff main...HEAD -- tagpu tools | grep '^+' | grep -oE '0x0*[45][0-9A-Fa-f]{5}'`
    is empty), so exe-reverse-engineering.md takes nothing either.
  - NO NEW GL OBJECT. The landing REMOVES a GL header dependency from three
    files; it creates nothing.
  - NOTHING MOVED. Function inventory diffed per translation unit against main:
    0 gone, 0 added.

WHAT IT DOES. `tagpu_fps.c`, `tagpu_scaffold.c` and `tagpu_render3do.c` included
`opengl_utils.h` while making no GL call. The include is gone from all three;
files including the header go nine -> six. The GL CALL surface is unchanged at
252/366 over four files (`tools/gl-sites.py`) -- a dependency removed, not a call.

THE ONE CHANGE THAT IS NOT A DELETION, and why it is safe by inspection rather
than by review: `tagpu_render3do.c` held the header with a single TYPE,

    GLuint tagpu_r3d_atlas_rgbref(void) { return s_atlas.rgb; }

`GLuint` is `typedef unsigned int` (inc/glcorearb.h:87); `tagpu_render3do.h:14`
ALREADY declared the function `unsigned int`; and the field returned is
`unsigned int` (tagpu_gaf.h). The definition was the odd spelling out. The object
file is the proof, not the argument: `_tagpu_r3d_atlas_rgbref` is still `T` in
tagpu_render3do.o and still `U` in its one caller tagpu_posedraw.o.

THE FIND, which is why this was not the trivial landing the row implied: A CALL
COUNT OF ZERO DOES NOT MEAN A FILE IS FREE OF THE HEADER. Of the five files with
no GL call, only two named nothing from it. `render_gdi.c` reads a VARIABLE
(`g_oglu_version`); `tagpu_gaf.c` takes three types, an entry-point resolver and
a calling convention; `tagpu_render3do.c` took one type in one place. The include
test therefore has to be a separate question from the call count, and it has to
be run on the RAW text -- an `#include` is itself a string literal, so a
comment/string mask deletes every include in the file.

AND THE PLAN'S OWN ROW WAS WRONG IN BOTH HALVES. It named `render_gdi.c` as
residue -- it is not, and the same page's "files it ends at" list already said so,
naming `render_gdi.c`'s `g_oglu_version` among the surviving plumbing users, so
the row and the list contradicted each other. And it said 11-5e-1 had taken
`tagpu_render3do.c`'s include; 11-5e-1 took a `tagpu_overlay.h` include out of
`tagpu_native.c`, a different file and a different header. Both corrected in the
plan and the roadmap by this landing.

A PRE-EXISTING WARNING FIXED, and established as pre-existing rather than assumed:
`tagpu_scaffold.c` had four clamps sharing two lines -- correct C, which -Wall
reads as -Wmisleading-indentation. Compiling MAIN's own copy of the file
reproduces both warnings, so they are not this landing's; they were invisible only
because the object was cached and this is the first landing to rebuild that file
in a while. One clamp per line now, which is the same code.

VERIFIED. Build gate: `thread-split: clean -- 34 listed file(s)`,
`spirv: 49 shaders, 33 programs, headers current`, exit 0, ZERO warnings.
Inventory 0/0. Wiki 63 pages + index.

NOT CLOSED. `opengl_utils.h` itself stays, with six includers -- four behind
escalation reason 1 (tagpu_hires_draw.c 104, tagpu_shadow.c 87, opengl_utils.c 31,
tagpu_hires.c 30). `tagpu_gaf.c`'s twelve wide sites go with 11-5e-2b. And
`tagpu_r3d_atlas_rgbref` is still an accessor over a field 11-5e-2 pinned to 0 --
retiring it is 11-5e-2b's; this landing only corrected its spelling.
